---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { collection } from '../../lib/content';
const notes = collection('notes').sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : -1));
---
<BaseLayout title="Notes — Freighthub Editorial Analysis" description="Original editorial analysis of China–GCC freight, customs and port economics, with judgement clearly separated from sourced fact.">
  <h1>Notes</h1>
  <p class="article-meta">Original analysis. These are arguments, not news: each note takes a position and shows the reasoning. Regulatory facts are sourced; the judgement is ours.</p>
  <ul class="note-list">
    {notes.map((n) => (
      <li>
        <p class="article-meta">{n.date} · {n.author || 'Freighthub editorial'}</p>
        <h2><a href={`/notes/${n.slug}/`}>{n.title}</a></h2>
        <p>{n.description}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>Our standards are in <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>; fixes are logged in <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>.</p>
  <h2>How to read a note</h2>
  <p>
    A note is an argument with its evidence attached. The regulatory facts are linked; the judgement
    is labelled as judgement and carries an exception — the case in which the author would not hold
    the position. Notes are dated because the argument can age even when the facts do not.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
